// Expanded lessons for the six thin topics — 9 new lessons each (3 + 9 = 12 per topic).
// Original, custom teaching content in the same {topic, title, body} format as the rest.
const LESSONS_EXPAND = [
  // ================= PEDIATRIC NEPHROLOGY (add 9) =================
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Normal Fluid and Electrolyte Balance in Children","body":"Children handle fluids and electrolytes differently from adults because of their higher body water percentage, higher surface-area-to-weight ratio, and immature renal function. A full-term infant's total body water is roughly 75 percent of body weight, compared with about 60 percent in an adult. This reservoir of water makes infants vulnerable to rapid dehydration when intake falls or losses rise, and equally prone to fluid overload if replacement is aggressive. Infant kidneys have a limited concentrating ability, so the urine cannot conserve water as effectively; this is why children need relatively more water per kilogram and can become hyponatremic, hypernatremic, or dehydrated more quickly. The nurse weighs the child daily, tracks strict intake and output including diaper weights, and watches for subtle signs of imbalance such as sunken fontanelles, dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, or decreased urine output. A weight change of one gram roughly equals one milliliter of fluid, so careful daily weights are a cornerstone of monitoring. Maintenance fluids in pediatrics are calculated by the Holliday-Segar method: 100 mL per kilogram for the first ten kilograms, 50 mL per kilogram for the next ten kilograms, and 20 mL per kilogram for each kilogram above twenty. Rapid correction of sodium disorders is dangerous because the immature brain is more prone to osmotic shifts; hypernatremia is corrected slowly over 24 to 48 hours to avoid cerebral edema. Dehydration severity is graded as mild, moderate, or severe, and the provider chooses oral rehydration for mild cases versus intravenous therapy for moderate to severe losses. The most common electrolyte disturbance in the hospitalized child is hyponatremia, often from hypotonic maintenance fluids or the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH). The nephrology nurse recognizes that children with preexisting renal disease, on diuretics, or with heart failure are at highest risk for sodium and potassium imbalance and requires more frequent laboratory monitoring."},
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Congenital Anomalies of the Kidney and Urinary Tract","body":"Congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract, abbreviated CAKUT, represent the most common cause of pediatric end-stage kidney disease and occur in about 1 in 500 births. The spectrum includes unilateral or bilateral renal agenesis, renal hypoplasia or dysplasia, hydronephrosis, posterior urethral valves, ureteropelvic junction obstruction, vesicoureteral reflux, and multicystic dysplastic kidney. Many of these are detected prenatally on obstetric ultrasound, which allows early planning and, in severe cases, intervention shortly after birth. Posterior urethral valves occur only in boys and can cause severe bladder outlet obstruction with bilateral hydronephrosis, requiring early valve ablation to preserve renal function. Vesicoureteral reflux, the retrograde flow of urine from the bladder toward the kidneys, predisposes to pyelonephritis and renal scarring; management ranges from observation and antibiotic prophylaxis for low grades to endoscopic injection or ureteral reimplantation for high grades. Multicystic dysplastic kidney is usually unilateral and often involutes spontaneously, but the contralateral kidney must be followed for compensatory hypertrophy and function. Renal function in children is best assessed by estimated glomerular filtration rate using the Bedside Schwartz equation, which incorporates serum creatinine and height. Children with a single functioning kidney should avoid contact sports and repeated high-impact trauma, and they need routine blood pressure and urine protein screening. Long-term risks include hypertension, proteinuria, reduced nephron mass, and progressive chronic kidney disease, especially in those with bilateral disease or obstruction. The nephrology nurse educates families about the importance of regular nephrology follow-up, blood pressure control, and infection prevention. Early identification and multidisciplinary management of CAKUT have improved long-term outcomes, but many children still require dialysis or transplantation by young adulthood."},
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Pediatric Urinary Tract Infections and Renal Scarring","body":"Urinary tract infection is one of the most common bacterial infections in children and is a leading reason for pediatric evaluation. Escherichia coli causes most infections, followed by other gram-negative organisms. In infants and young children, fever may be the only symptom, along with irritability, poor feeding, vomiting, or lethargy; classic dysuria and frequency appear in older, toilet-trained children. Because young children cannot localize symptoms, a febrile urinary tract infection in an infant is presumed to involve the upper tract, and prompt treatment is essential to prevent renal scarring. The diagnosis relies on a properly collected urine specimen, ideally a clean-catch midstream sample in older children or catheterized sampling in infants and non-toilet-trained children, with culture confirming the organism. Urinalysis showing pyuria and bacteriuria supports the diagnosis, and a positive culture with a significant colony count confirms it. Antibiotic treatment should be initiated promptly after culture collection, guided by the organism and local resistance patterns. Recurrent infections, a family history of vesicoureteral reflux, renal scarring, hypertension, or prenatal hydronephrosis warrant further imaging, most commonly a renal bladder ultrasound and a voiding cystourethrogram to detect reflux. The goal of evaluation is to identify reflux and structural abnormalities that predispose to pyelonephritis and scarring. Long-term consequences of recurrent febrile infections include renal scarring, which can progress to hypertension and reduced glomerular filtration rate over time. The nephrology nurse emphasizes prevention: adequate hydration, regular voiding, avoidance of constipation, and correct wiping technique in girls. Antibiotic prophylaxis is reserved for children with high-grade reflux or frequent breakthrough infections. Families are taught to recognize symptoms early and seek prompt care rather than waiting, because early treatment is the best protection against permanent kidney damage."},
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Nephrotic Syndrome: Complications and Nursing Management","body":"Nephrotic syndrome in children is defined by heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia. The pediatric form is usually minimal change disease, which is highly steroid-sensitive, but complications can be serious and require careful nursing vigilance. The most feared complications are infection, thromboembolism, and acute kidney injury. Protein losses deplete immunoglobulins and complement, increasing susceptibility to serious infections such as peritonitis, cellulitis, and bacteremia, most commonly from encapsulated organisms like Streptococcus pneumoniae. The nurse watches for fever, abdominal pain, or new swelling that may signal bacterial peritonitis and alerts the provider urgently. Anticoagulant proteins are also lost in the urine while prothrombotic factors are preserved, creating a hypercoagulable state with risk for renal vein thrombosis and other thromboembolic events, so unexplained abdominal pain, an enlarging kidney, or a new swollen limb warrants investigation. The child is edematous because low oncotic pressure drives fluid into the interstitial space; management includes limiting sodium, careful monitoring of urine output and daily weights, and judicious use of diuretics, since aggressive diuresis can precipitate hypovolemia and acute kidney injury while the child is already depleted of intravascular volume. Steroids remain the first-line treatment for minimal change disease, and the family needs clear teaching about the importance of completing the full course and monitoring for steroid side effects such as mood changes, hyperglycemia, and growth suppression. Some children are steroid-resistant or frequently relapsing and may require calcineurin inhibitors, cyclophosphamide, or rituximab. Routine immunizations, especially pneumococcal vaccine, are important but live vaccines are generally held during high-dose immunosuppression. The nurse also addresses psychosocial needs, because steroid dependence and chronic disease affect body image and school attendance. With modern therapy most children with minimal change disease have an excellent long-term outlook, but the complications around each relapse demand meticulous monitoring."},
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Acute Glomerulonephritis in Children","body":"Acute glomerulonephritis in children presents with the sudden onset of hematuria, proteinuria, hypertension, and reduced urine output, often with periorbital and peripheral edema. The most common poststreptococcal form follows a streptococcal throat or skin infection by one to three weeks and is mediated by immune complexes that deposit in the glomeruli, triggering inflammation. Children typically present with dark or cola-colored urine, edema, and elevated blood pressure, though symptoms can range from asymptomatic microscopic hematuria to nephrotic-range proteinuria and acute kidney injury. The diagnosis is supported by a recent history of streptococcal infection, an elevated antistreptolysin O titer, low serum complement, and the characteristic urinalysis showing red blood cell casts. Blood pressure control is the cornerstone of acute management because hypertension can cause hypertensive encephalopathy, seizures, and further renal injury; antihypertensives such as calcium channel blockers, and diuretics for fluid overload, are used as indicated. The nurse monitors vital signs frequently, tracks strict intake and output, and restricts sodium and fluids during the oliguric phase. Severe pulmonary edema and hypertensive crisis are emergencies that require urgent intervention. Most children with poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis recover fully with supportive care, and the prognosis is excellent, though a minority progress to chronic kidney disease. Other causes of glomerulonephritis in children include IgA nephropathy, which often follows a viral upper respiratory infection with gross hematuria, and IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schoenlein purpura), which adds a purpuric rash, abdominal pain, and arthralgia. Complement levels help distinguish poststreptococcal disease, where complement is low and eventually normalizes, from other causes. The nephrology nurse educates families on blood pressure and weight monitoring at home, adherence to follow-up, and prompt reporting of recurrence. Long-term surveillance includes serial urinalysis, blood pressure checks, and kidney function tests even after apparent recovery."},
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Hypertension in Children: Assessment and Initial Management","body":"Hypertension is increasingly recognized in children, usually discovered during routine examination. Primary hypertension is more common in older children, particularly those who are overweight or have a family history, while secondary hypertension is more likely in younger children and often points to renal, renovascular, or endocrine causes. Renal parenchymal disease is the leading cause of secondary hypertension in children, so evaluation includes a urinalysis, serum creatinine, electrolytes, and a renal ultrasound. Blood pressure in children must be interpreted against age-, sex-, and height-specific percentiles rather than fixed adult thresholds; elevated readings require confirmation with repeated measurements and appropriate cuff size. Hypertension is staged as elevated, stage 1, or stage 2 based on the percentile, and ambulatory blood pressure monitoring is used to confirm the diagnosis and detect white-coat hypertension. Initial management emphasizes lifestyle: weight reduction if overweight, dietary changes with a reduced sodium intake, regular aerobic activity, and limited screen time. Pharmacologic therapy is reserved for symptomatic hypertension, secondary hypertension, or persistent elevation after lifestyle measures. Amlodipine and lisinopril are common choices, with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors favored in the setting of proteinuria or diabetes. The most dangerous complication is hypertensive encephalopathy, presenting with headache, visual disturbance, or seizures, which is a medical emergency. The nephrology nurse measures blood pressure correctly in the right arm, uses an appropriately sized cuff, avoids caffeine before reading, and educates families about home blood pressure monitoring, medication adherence, and the importance of avoiding nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, which can raise pressure and worsen renal function. Undertreated hypertension silently damages the heart, blood vessels, and kidneys over years, so early recognition and consistent follow-up are essential. Long-term follow-up focuses on blood pressure control, monitoring for proteinuria, and preserving kidney function."},
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Dialysis Options for Children: HD, PD, and CRRT","body":"When children reach end-stage kidney disease, renal replacement therapy options include peritoneal dialysis, hemodialysis, and kidney transplantation, with transplant being the preferred long-term therapy. Peritoneal dialysis is the most common modality for young children because it requires no vascular access, is gentler on hemodynamics, and can be performed at home by caregivers, preserving a more normal daily routine and enabling school attendance. Challenges in infant PD include the need for careful fluid and calorie management to support growth and the risk of peritonitis from caregiver technique. Hemodialysis requires reliable vascular access, which is more difficult in small children; central venous catheters are often used because veins are too small for fistulas, and AV fistulas are reserved for older children or adolescents. Hemodialysis in children demands precise ultrafiltration to avoid hypotension and a careful assessment of dry weight, which changes rapidly with growth. In the acute setting, continuous kidney replacement therapy (CRRT) is used for critically ill children with acute kidney injury, fluid overload, or metabolic instability, providing gradual solute and fluid removal that tolerates a vulnerable hemodynamic state. Pediatric CRRT requires specialized circuits, smaller tubing to minimize blood priming volume, and close monitoring of the child who may need priming with blood products to prevent hypovolemia. Anticoagulation in children must balance circuit patency against bleeding risk, and regional citrate is increasingly preferred. The interdisciplinary team, including nephrology, nursing, dietitians, child life specialists, and social workers, supports the child and family throughout. Growth and development are major concerns, so nutrition is optimized to provide adequate calories and protein while controlling potassium, phosphorus, and fluid. Vaccination status, psychosocial support, and school reintegration are important components of care. The nephrology nurse coordinates with the transplant team from the start, because preparation for transplant while on dialysis improves outcomes."},
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Nutritional Considerations in Pediatric Kidney Disease","body":"Nutrition is foundational in pediatric kidney disease because growth failure is one of the most common and serious complications, and it directly affects morbidity and long-term outcomes. Children with chronic kidney disease frequently have reduced appetite, nausea, and altered taste, making it hard to meet their higher energy and protein needs for growth. The diet must deliver enough calories to support linear growth while controlling the metabolic consequences of renal failure, including potassium, phosphorus, and sodium retention. Protein is provided at age-appropriate levels, generally at the upper range of recommended intake for the growing child, while avoiding excess that worsens uremia. Phosphorus is restricted because hyperphosphatemia drives secondary hyperparathyroidism and bone disease, and phosphate binders are given with meals to control absorption. Potassium intake may need to be limited in children with decreased urine output or on dialysis, though infants on PD may actually require potassium supplementation because dialysis removes it. Sodium is restricted to manage blood pressure and fluid balance, and fluids are adjusted based on residual urine output and dialysis modality. Growth is monitored by tracking height and weight on standard growth curves, and the use of recombinant human growth hormone is considered in growth-hypocrine children who fail to respond to nutritional measures. Children on dialysis lose amino acids and protein into the dialysate, so their protein needs are higher; those on peritoneal dialysis lose protein daily into the effluent and may need supplementation. Micronutrient supplementation, including water-soluble vitamins and iron, is often required because losses exceed intake. Tube feeding is frequently used in infants and young children to reliably deliver calories and reach growth goals. The dietitian works closely with the nephrology nurse and family to create practical, culturally appropriate meal plans, manage tube feeding, and address food preferences to improve intake. Malnutrition, inflammation, and disease burden interact to retard growth, so a proactive, multidisciplinary approach is essential."},
  {"topic":"Pediatric Nephrology","title":"Transitioning the Young Adult with Kidney Disease to Adult Care","body":"Transition is the planned, structured process of moving an adolescent or young adult with chronic kidney disease from pediatric to adult healthcare services. Young people with CKD, on dialysis, or with a transplant face this shift at a vulnerable time, and a poorly managed transition is linked to worsening adherence, graft loss after transplant, missed appointments, and higher mortality. A successful transition begins early—ideally starting around age twelve to fourteen—and proceeds gradually through three phases: preparation, transfer, and integration into adult care. During preparation the youth is assessed for readiness, including knowledge of the diagnosis, medications, dialysis or transplant details, and independence in self-management skills. The nephrology nurse teaches the young adult to understand their condition, track medications and refills, keep a symptom diary, and communicate directly with the healthcare team rather than relying solely on parents. Practical skills such as ordering supplies, scheduling appointments, and understanding insurance are introduced and practiced. The transfer to adult care is coordinated, with a defined handoff that summarizes the clinical history, current medications, dialysis or graft status, and psychosocial needs, and the adult clinic is chosen based on the patient's preferences and proximity. Integration includes confirming the first adult appointment is kept, verifying the transfer of records, and ensuring the young adult knows who to contact with urgent concerns. Common challenges are loss of parental support for logistics, differences in style between pediatric and adult clinics, and adolescent risk behavior, so ongoing education on contraception, substance use, and medication adherence is essential. Adult care emphasizes shared decision-making and patient-centered goals. The nurse assesses transition skills at each visit, uses transition readiness tools, and collaborates with social work and child life to address barriers such as financial stress and mental health. A structured, well-timed transition program improves long-term outcomes, reduces graft loss, and supports independence."},
  // ================= PREGNANCY AND KIDNEY DISEASE (add 9) =================
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Renal Function Monitoring and Urinalysis in Pregnancy","body":"Monitoring renal function in pregnancy requires attention to the normal physiologic changes that alter laboratory values. Serum creatinine falls in pregnancy because of increased glomerular filtration, so a value that is normal for a nonpregnant woman may represent reduced function in a pregnant patient. A creatinine of 0.8 mg/dL or higher in pregnancy is often regarded as abnormal and warrants nephrology evaluation. The estimated glomerular filtration rate equations used in nonpregnant adults are not reliable in pregnancy, so clinicians follow serum creatinine trends and the clinical picture rather than a single eGFR number. Urinalysis is central to surveillance because proteinuria is a key marker of preeclampsia and of progressive renal disease. A clean-catch urine sample is collected to distinguish true proteinuria from contamination, and the dipstick is confirmed with a quantitative measure such as a spot urine protein-to-creatinine ratio or a 24-hour urine collection. Because renal function normally improves early in pregnancy and returns toward baseline later, a rising creatinine or worsening proteinuria signals complications that need investigation. Blood pressure should be measured at every prenatal visit with an appropriately sized cuff and the patient seated, because hypertension often appears first and rises the fastest in the third trimester. The nurse reviews the obstetric record, tracks serial weights and blood pressure trends, and watches for symptoms such as headache, visual changes, or right upper quadrant pain that suggest preeclampsia. Baseline renal imaging is usually deferred unless indicated, because the enlarging uterus causes physiologic hydronephrosis that can be mistaken for obstruction. Medication review is essential, because many antihypertensives and immunosuppressants have teratogenic risk, and dosing may need adjustment as renal clearance changes. Regular communication between the obstetrician, nephrologist, and nephrology nurse ensures that renal indices, fluid balance, and fetal well-being are monitored together. The ultimate goals are to preserve maternal renal function, detect preeclampsia early, and optimize fetal outcomes through coordinated multidisciplinary care."},
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Chronic Kidney Disease and Prepregnancy Counseling","body":"For a woman with chronic kidney disease who is considering pregnancy, careful prepregnancy counseling is essential because pregnancy stress can accelerate the decline of renal function and because maternal kidney disease poses risks to the fetus. The key determinants of maternal and fetal outcomes are the baseline kidney function, the degree of proteinuria, and the presence of hypertension. Women with mild kidney disease, defined by preserved glomerular filtration rate and minimal proteinuria, generally have good pregnancy outcomes, whereas those with advanced CKD, nephrotic-range proteinuria, or uncontrolled hypertension face higher rates of preeclampsia, preterm delivery, low birth weight, and a more rapid decline in renal function. Pregnancy in a woman on dialysis is difficult but possible, and pregnancy after kidney transplant is cautiously encouraged after a stable post-transplant period. Prepregnancy evaluation includes assessment of renal function, blood pressure, proteinuria, and review of all medications to identify those that are teratogenic, such as angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, mycophenolate, and cyclophosphamide, which should be changed to safer alternatives before conception. Folic acid supplementation is advised to reduce neural tube defects, and vaccination status is updated before pregnancy. Counseling is shared decision-making that honestly presents the risks of disease progression, preeclampsia, preterm birth, and fetal loss while acknowledging that many pregnancies succeed, especially in women with mild disease who are well controlled. The plan covers the timing of nephrology and high-risk obstetric visits, the frequency of monitoring, target blood pressure and proteinuria goals, and the plan for delivery. Contraception is discussed for women who do not wish to conceive and for those in whom pregnancy would be hazardous. A multidisciplinary team including nephrology, maternal-fetal medicine, nursing, and pharmacy coordinates care through preconception, pregnancy, and the postpartum period. The nephrology nurse reinforces blood pressure monitoring, dietary guidance, medication safety, and early reporting of concerning symptoms, and supports the woman through the emotional challenges of a high-risk pregnancy."},
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Managing Hypertension in Pregnancy with Kidney Disease","body":"Hypertension complicates pregnancy in women with chronic kidney disease and requires careful management to protect both mother and fetus. The goal is to control severe hypertension and prevent maternal complications while maintaining sufficient placental perfusion for fetal growth, so treatment balances blood pressure targets against the risk of reduced uterine blood flow. Chronic hypertension in pregnancy is defined as hypertension that predates the pregnancy or is diagnosed before twenty weeks of gestation, whereas preeclampsia develops after twenty weeks with proteinuria or evidence of end-organ dysfunction. In a woman with kidney disease, the distinction can be difficult because CKD itself may cause hypertension and proteinuria, so a rising blood pressure with new or worsening proteinuria late in pregnancy suggests superimposed preeclampsia. Antihypertensive medications are chosen for fetal safety. Labetalol and nifedipine are commonly used, and methyldopa is an older option, while angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers are contraindicated in pregnancy because of fetal renal and developmental toxicity; they are typically discontinued before or at the time of conception and replaced with a pregnancy-safe agent. Severe acute hypertension, defined by a blood pressure of 160/110 mmHg or higher, is treated rapidly, often with intravenous labetalol or hydralazine, to prevent maternal stroke and placental insufficiency. The nurse monitors blood pressure frequently in both arms, tracks symptoms such as headache, visual disturbances, and epigastric pain, and measures urine protein and fetal heart rate. The timing of delivery is individualized based on gestational age, blood pressure control, proteinuria, and fetal well-being, with delivery indicated when maternal or fetal risk outweighs the risks of prematurity. Postpartum blood pressure is followed closely because hypertension can worsen after delivery. The nurse educates the patient on home blood pressure monitoring, medication timing, and when to seek emergency care, and emphasizes that elevated blood pressure in pregnancy increases long-term cardiovascular and renal risk, warranting ongoing follow-up after delivery."},
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Acute Kidney Injury in Pregnancy: Causes and Management","body":"Acute kidney injury in pregnancy is uncommon in developed countries but represents a serious obstetric emergency. The most important causes are preeclampsia and eclampsia, HELLP syndrome, obstetric hemorrhage with hypovolemia, sepsis, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy. Less common causes include urinary obstruction from the enlarging uterus, and specific pregnancy-related disorders such as thrombotic microangiopathy. The clinical presentation includes rising serum creatinine, reduced urine output, and depending on the cause, hypertension, proteinuria, hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets typical of HELLP syndrome. Prompt recognition is critical because several of these conditions are rapidly reversible if the cause is addressed. Management is primarily supportive and centered on treating the underlying obstetric condition. Severe preeclampsia or HELLP syndrome usually improves with delivery, while volume loss from hemorrhage is corrected with fluids and blood products, and sepsis is treated with appropriate antibiotics and source control. The role of dialysis in pregnancy is to manage refractory fluid overload, severe metabolic acidosis, or uremia when medical management is insufficient; hemodialysis is preferred, with adjustments for the increased intravascular volume and metabolic demands of pregnancy. Hypovolemia is a risk with ultrafiltration, so dialysis is performed carefully. The nurse monitors maternal vital signs, urine output, renal indices, and fetal heart rate, and coordinates with maternal-fetal medicine. Acute kidney injury that does not resolve can progress to chronic kidney disease or end-stage renal disease, so long-term follow-up of renal function, blood pressure, and proteinuria is warranted after recovery. Education focuses on early reporting of warning signs such as severe headache, visual changes, edema, or reduced fetal movement, and adherence to antenatal visits. Multidisciplinary coordination between obstetric and nephrology services improves outcomes in this high-risk condition."},
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Hemodialysis in Pregnancy: Prescription and Monitoring","body":"Pregnant women on maintenance hemodialysis present unique challenges because pregnancy increases dialysis requirements and alters the usual targets. Fetal outcomes are markedly improved when dialysis is intensified, so the common goal is to increase dialysis time and frequency, often to five or six sessions per week, to reduce uremia, maintain better volume control, and optimize nutrition. This intensive schedule is associated with better fetal growth and survival, higher birth weights, and lower rates of preterm delivery compared with standard thrice-weekly dialysis. Because the normal pregnancy expands maternal extracellular volume and increases metabolic demands, the dialysis prescription is adjusted for a gradual weight gain, and dry weight is reassessed frequently to accommodate the growing uterus and fluid shifts. Ultrafiltration is often minimized to protect placental perfusion and avoid hypotension and hypovolemia, which can compromise the fetus, so interdialytic weight gains are monitored closely and the patient is counseled on limiting sodium and fluid intake. Medication and electrolyte goals change in pregnancy; for example, dialysis may remove nutrients and medications, so supplementation of water-soluble vitamins, calcium, and possibly higher target serum bicarbonate is considered. Antihypertensives are adjusted to pregnancy-safe agents, with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and receptor blockers avoided. Heparin anticoagulation during dialysis is generally considered safe in pregnancy and does not cross the placenta in significant amounts, but the dose is minimized. Continuous fetal monitoring and serial ultrasound assessment of fetal growth and amniotic fluid index are used to guide management. The nephrology nurse coordinates closely with maternal-fetal medicine, tracks blood pressure before, during, and after each treatment, monitors for cramping or signs of uterine contraction, and reports any vaginal bleeding or fluid leakage immediately, as it may indicate preterm labor, which is more common in this population. Dietary guidance emphasizes adequate protein and calories to support both fetal growth and the losses of dialysis."},
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Peritoneal Dialysis in Pregnancy","body":"Peritoneal dialysis is feasible in pregnancy, though it requires close monitoring and coordinated obstetric and nephrology care. Because pregnancy increases peritoneal surface area demand and metabolic clearance needs, many centers recommend more frequent, smaller-volume exchanges rather than a large single dwell, which can cause discomfort and impede uterine growth. The increased intra-abdominal pressure from the gravid uterus can raise the risk of catheter problems, fluid leaks, and hernias, and the choice of exchange volume is individualized to balance clearance and comfort. Ultrafiltration and volume status are monitored carefully because pregnancy requires gradual volume expansion for fetal growth, so the goal is to avoid both hypovolemia and fluid overload while allowing appropriate weight gain. Serum electrolyte targets and the adequacy of clearance, reflected in Kt/V, are followed; dialysis adequacy targets may not be fully met in pregnancy, so clinical, biochemical, and fetal parameters guide therapy. Peritonitis risk is a concern, and meticulous aseptic technique is reinforced. Because protein is lost in the dialysate, protein intake is increased to meet the needs of both the fetus and the peritoneal losses, and water-soluble vitamins may need supplementation. Blood pressure and proteinuria are monitored to detect superimposed preeclampsia, and antihypertensives are limited to pregnancy-safe agents. The nephrology nurse teaches the pregnant woman to perform exchanges comfortably by adjusting position, to recognize early signs of infection or preterm labor, and to report symptoms such as leaking dialysate, abdominal pain, or contractions promptly. Delivery is usually planned near term when possible given the obstetric status. Multidisciplinary teams optimize maternal health, dialysis adequacy, and fetal growth. With careful planning many women on peritoneal dialysis achieve successful pregnancies and healthy infants, and ongoing follow-up of renal function continues postpartum."},
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Eclampsia: Seizure Prevention and Emergency Care","body":"Eclampsia is the occurrence of generalized seizures in a woman with preeclampsia and represents a life-threatening emergency for both mother and fetus. It can occur before, during, or after delivery, and it is considered hypertensive emergency of pregnancy. The priority is to stop the seizure, protect the airway, and prevent maternal injury and fetal compromise while expediting delivery of the fetus when appropriate. Magnesium sulfate is the standard agent to prevent and treat eclamptic seizures, and it is used both prophylactically in severe preeclampsia and to control recurrent seizures in eclampsia. The nurse administers a magnesium sulfate bolus and maintenance infusion, monitors for magnesium toxicity with serial assessment of deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output, and is prepared to reverse toxicity with intravenous calcium gluconate if reflexes are lost or the respiratory rate falls. Blood pressure is controlled to reduce the risk of stroke and further seizures, and the airway is maintained with positioning and suction while preventing aspiration. Continuous maternal blood pressure, oxygen saturation, and fetal heart rate monitoring are essential, and the delivery of the fetus is usually the definitive treatment, with the timing based on gestational age and severity. Nursing care includes turning the patient, padding the bed, keeping the environment calm, and providing emotional support to the frightened patient and family. After a seizure, the patient is monitored closely in a high-acuity setting for recurrent seizures, pulmonary edema, and stroke, and postpartum surveillance continues because seizures can still occur in the first days after delivery. Long-term, hypertension and proteinuria are followed, and the woman is educated that a history of eclampsia increases future cardiovascular and renal risk. The nephrology nurse reinforces blood pressure control, medication adherence, and the importance of preconception counseling in any future pregnancy."},
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Thrombotic Microangiopathies in Pregnancy","body":"Pregnancy can trigger or unmask thrombotic microangiopathies, disorders characterized by microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and organ injury from microvascular thrombosis. The differential includes thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS), and HELLP syndrome, and distinguishing them matters because treatments differ. TTP is caused by severe deficiency of ADAMTS13, the enzyme that cleaves von Willebrand factor, and responds to plasma exchange; features include neurologic symptoms and fever. Pregnancy-associated HUS, often related to the alternative complement pathway, tends to occur postpartum and does not reliably respond to plasma exchange, so complement inhibition with agents such as eculizumab may be used. HELLP syndrome is a severe form of preeclampsia defined by hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets, and is managed primarily by delivery. Patients may present with anemia, bruising, renal dysfunction, hypertension, and laboratory evidence of red cell fragmentation, so the nurse reviews the complete blood count, peripheral smear, lactate dehydrogenase, bilirubin, and kidney function. Because the complications overlap, prompt specialist evaluation is essential and empiric plasma exchange is often considered urgently while awaiting confirmatory testing. General supportive care includes blood pressure control, correction of electrolyte and fluid disturbances, platelet and transfusion support only as indicated, and maintenance of renal function. Acute kidney injury is common and may require dialysis. The nephrology nurse monitors serial labs, urine output, and neurologic status, and coordinates with maternal-fetal medicine, nephrology, and hematology. Because these conditions carry significant maternal mortality, early recognition and rapid escalation of care are vital. Long-term follow-up includes monitoring for recovery of renal function, and genetic counseling is offered when an inherited complement abnormality is identified, since recurrence risk impacts future pregnancies."},
  {"topic":"Pregnancy and Kidney Disease","title":"Medication Review for the Pregnant Renal Patient","body":"Medication management in the pregnancy with kidney disease requires a careful review to balance maternal treatment needs with fetal safety. Several classes used in nephrology are teratogenic and are changed before or at the time of conception. Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers are avoided in pregnancy because they can cause fetal renal hypoplasia, oligohydramnios, and limb and skull abnormalities, especially in the second and third trimesters, and they are replaced with labetalol, nifedipine, or methyldopa. Immunosuppressants require adjustment: mycophenolate is contraindicated because of a high risk of miscarriage and congenital malformations, cyclophosphamide is avoided, and prednisone is used cautiously, while many centers allow tacrolimus at targeted levels for women with a kidney transplant, monitoring closely. Phosphate binders, erythropoiesis-stimulating agents, and diuretics are reviewed, with diuretics used sparingly because they may reduce placental perfusion. The dosing of drugs cleared by the kidney may need adjustment as renal function changes through pregnancy, so serum drug levels are monitored for agents with a narrow therapeutic index. The nurse obtains a complete medication list at each visit, confirms the indication and dose of every agent, verifies that teratogenic drugs have been changed, and educates the patient to consult her provider before starting any new medication, over-the-counter product, or herbal supplement. Antihypertensive blood pressure goals and the plan for anticoagulation, if indicated, are documented, and the patient is taught to report side effects. A coordinated approach includes the pharmacist, nephrologist, obstetrician, and nephrology nurse to maintain therapeutic efficacy and fetal safety. The patient is counseled about the importance of continued medication adherence for conditions such as hypertension and after transplant, because stopping immunosuppression can precipitate rejection. This vigilant medication review, beginning in the preconception period and continuing through postpartum, is essential to optimize outcomes."},
  // ================= DRUG DOSING IN RENAL FAILURE (add 9) =================
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"Pharmacokinetics in Chronic Kidney Disease","body":"Chronic kidney disease alters every phase of drug handling, which is why medication dosing must be adjusted as renal function declines. Absorption can be changed by uremia-induced nausea, gastroparesis, altered gastric pH from antacids, and edema of the gastrointestinal tract; several oral drugs have reduced or unpredictable absorption. Distribution is altered by changes in body water, protein binding, and acidemia, so the volume of distribution of water-soluble and highly protein-bound drugs shifts, affecting the loading dose. Metabolism in the liver and gut is affected as well, because uremic toxins and inflammation inhibit some hepatic enzymes and transporters, meaning that even drugs primarily metabolized by the liver may accumulate. Excretion is the most predictable change, since a decline in glomerular filtration reduces the renal clearance of drugs and their active or toxic metabolites. As a result, many medications require a reduced maintenance dose, a longer dosing interval, or both to avoid accumulation and toxicity. Drugs with a narrow therapeutic index, such as digoxin, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, and lithium, pose the highest risk and require therapeutic drug monitoring. The half-life of renally cleared drugs increases, so the time to steady state lengthens and the peak levels after a dose rise; therefore clinicians often prolong the dosing interval rather than merely lower each dose, to avoid repeated supratherapeutic peaks. Loading doses generally do not need to be reduced because they distribute into an unchanged volume, but maintenance doses are adjusted according to estimated renal function using formulas such as the Cockcroft-Gault or CKD-EPI estimation, recognizing that these are estimates of creatinine clearance. The nurse assesses renal function before dosing, reviews the prescribed dose against the patient's eGFR, and, for high-risk drugs, checks serum levels. Drug errors in renal impairment are preventable when prescriptions are cross-checked against guidelines, and the patient is taught about signs of toxicity and the need to avoid nephrotoxic or renally accumulating drugs. Individualization, monitoring, and patient education are the cornerstones of safe medication use in chronic kidney disease."},
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"Renal Drug Dosing Equations and Their Use","body":"Estimating kidney function is essential for dosing renally cleared drugs, and clinicians use estimated creatinine clearance or estimated glomerular filtration rate as the basis. The Cockcroft-Gault equation estimates creatinine clearance from serum creatinine, age, weight, and sex, and has historically been the standard for drug dosing studies and many package inserts. The CKD-EPI equation estimates glomerular filtration rate normalized to body surface area and is widely reported by laboratories; it is preferred for classifying and staging chronic kidney disease, though drug-dosing guidance frequently refers to Cockcroft-Gault values. Because the two estimates can differ, especially at the extremes of age, body size, and muscle mass, the clinician must know which equation was used and match it to the dosing reference. In general, drug-dosing decisions rely on estimates of creatinine clearance more than on a single eGFR number, and renal function is assessed periodically because it changes with disease progression, acute illness, and medication use. Several online tools and renal drug reference databases use these equations and provide recommended dose adjustments based on renal function strata. Limitations include the inaccuracy of using serum creatinine in patients with low muscle mass, the elderly, amputees, and those with acute kidney injury, where creatinine lags behind actual changes in filtration. When an accurate measure is needed, a measured creatinine clearance from a timed urine collection or an alternative such as cystatin C may be used, particularly in patients with borderline function where dosing is critical. The nurse confirms which renal estimate is documented, ensures the body weight and serum creatinine are current, and flags patients whose renal function has changed so that doses may be reassessed. Dosing adjustments are individualized; for example, a drug with a wide safety margin may be given at a reduced interval, while a narrow-therapeutic-index drug requires a specific regimen and therapeutic monitoring. Communication between the prescriber, pharmacist, and nephrology nurse prevents dosing errors, and renal dosing guidelines are updated as newer drugs are approved. Understanding the basis of each estimate, its limitations, and when to obtain a measured clearance is central to safe prescribing."},
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"Antibiotics That Need Renal Dose Adjustment","body":"Many antibiotics are cleared by the kidney and require dose adjustment in chronic kidney disease and acute kidney injury to avoid accumulation and toxicity while maintaining efficacy. Aminoglycosides such as gentamicin and tobramycin are renally cleared and nephrotoxic and ototoxic; they require careful dosing, monitoring of peak and trough levels, and avoidance or cautious use with other nephrotoxins. Vancomycin is primarily cleared by the kidney, and its trough concentration and area under the curve must be monitored, with dosing based on renal function. Beta-lactams including many penicillins and cephalosporins are largely renally excreted, so doses or intervals are reduced in impaired renal function to prevent neurotoxicity and accumulation. Fluoroquinolones such as levofloxacin and ciprofloxacin require adjustment in renal impairment, and fluconazole dosing is adjusted based on renal function. Daptomycin clearance is partly renal and dose adjustments and monitoring are recommended. Aminoglycosides and some beta-lactams can accumulate, causing acute kidney injury on top of underlying renal disease, so the nurse reviews renal function before each dose, monitors kidney indices and serum drug levels, and reports signs of ototoxicity or nephrotoxicity. When a patient's renal function changes abruptly during an acute illness, dosing should be reassessed promptly because a previously adequate regimen can become toxic. In patients with acute kidney injury who are started on renal replacement therapy, the dosing is adjusted to account for drug removal by dialysis, and drugs removed by dialysis may need supplemental doses after a treatment. The safest approach is to consult renal dosing references, confirm the organism and source, and individualize based on the patient's estimated renal function, drug levels when available, and clinical response. The nurse also observes for allergic reactions and superinfection. Because underdosing in a patient with a serious infection can worsen outcome, careful attention to the interaction between renal function, drug dose, and monitoring is essential."},
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"Adjusting Doses for Peritoneal Dialysis","body":"Drug dosing in patients on peritoneal dialysis differs from hemodialysis because the peritoneal membrane clears solutes and drugs slowly based on their molecular size, protein binding, and the dialysis prescription. In general, peritoneal dialysis provides less drug clearance than hemodialysis, so many drugs are dosed as if the patient has severe chronic kidney disease with minimal renal elimination, with adjustments based on residual kidney function and the modality. Small, water-soluble, poorly protein-bound drugs may be removed in the dialysate and require supplemental dosing or a shortened interval, whereas large molecules and highly protein-bound drugs are minimally cleared by the peritoneum. The clearance of aminoglycosides by peritoneal dialysis is modest, so they are dosed carefully, and some antibiotics can be given by the intraperitoneal route, which delivers high local concentrations for the treatment of peritonitis. Vancomycin given intraperitoneally uses the dwell time to allow systemic absorption and is dosed based on measured serum levels and residual clearance. Drugs that accumulate in renal failure, such as lithium, need monitoring, because dialysis removal may be incomplete and the risk of toxicity remains significant. Because the peritoneal membrane characteristics vary between patients and with time, and because residual kidney function declines, drug dosing should be periodically assessed rather than assumed to be static. The nurse reviews the patient's modality, the current peritoneal equilibration test result, residual urine output, and the drug's properties, and monitors serum levels for drugs with a narrow therapeutic index. Dosing adjustments are made according to the peritoneal dialysis reference, and the patient is educated to recognize signs of drug toxicity. When peritonitis is treated, the dosing of intraperitoneal antibiotics is based on the dwell time and the volume, and antifungal prophylaxis is added. Coordinated care among the prescriber, pharmacist, and dialysis nurse ensures that the dose matches the patient's clearance, that drug levels are drawn correctly from the appropriate sites, and that adjustments account for both the peritoneal membrane and any residual renal function."},
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"Drug Dosing for Hemodialysis Patients","body":"In hemodialysis patients, drug dosing must account for removal during the dialysis treatment as well as for the substantial reduction in native renal clearance. Most drugs that are cleared by the kidney require dose adjustment, and the pattern depends on whether the drug is dialyzed off during a session. Drugs with small molecular size, low protein binding, and a small volume of distribution are more likely to be cleared by hemodialysis and may need a dose after dialysis to replace what is lost; a common practice is to administer the regular dose after the treatment, since predialysis dosing would be partially removed. Drugs that are large, protein-bound, or extensively distributed in tissues are minimally cleared by dialysis and are dosed as if the patient had severe renal failure without significant dialytic clearance, using the interdialytic period as the dosing baseline. Certain drugs with narrow therapeutic indices, such as aminoglycosides and vancomycin, require monitoring of serum levels that are drawn at a defined time relative to the dialysis session to give an accurate picture of the true concentration. Because hemodialysis provides periodic, not continuous, clearance, the goal is to avoid both toxic peaks, by reducing the maintenance dose or interval, and subtherapeutic troughs, by supplementing after dialysis when needed. The dialysis membrane and prescription, including blood flow and treatment duration, affect the extent of drug removal. The nurse confirms the timing of drug administration relative to dialysis, ensures that doses meant to be given after treatment are administered on the correct day, and documents the timing in the medication record. Residual renal function, when present, and the specific dialyzer are considered. The patient is educated about the importance of taking medications on schedule, and that some doses are timed around the dialysis session. Cross-checking the regimen with the pharmacist, monitoring drug levels, and reassessing when treatment frequency changes are essential to safe and effective medication use in the dialysis population."},
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"Drug Dosing During Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy","body":"Patients receiving continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT) have drug clearance determined by both residual kidney function and the continuous removal of solutes and drugs by the artificial circuit, making drug dosing quite different from intermittent hemodialysis. Because CRRT runs continuously, it clears drugs steadily, and many renally cleared drugs that accumulate over a long interval on intermittent hemodialysis may be cleared more effectively with CRRT, often permitting a more normal dosing approach while still reducing doses relative to normal renal function. The extent of clearance depends on the drug's molecular size, protein binding, volume of distribution, and the CRRT modality and effluent dose. Small, water-soluble, poorly protein-bound drugs are removed readily; large or highly protein-bound drugs are less affected. Drugs with a narrow therapeutic index, such as aminoglycosides and vancomycin, require monitoring of serum concentrations during CRRT because clearance can change as the effluent rate and circuit performance vary. When a patient starts CRRT, doses are often adjusted to an approximate clearance reflecting the circuit, and when CRRT is stopped, doses must be revised again because clearance drops considerably, so the nurse is alert to the moment the prescription changes and prompts a medication review. Nutrition, electrolytes, and many medications are managed proactively during CRRT. Citrate-based regional anticoagulation can also affect calcium and drug handling, so calcium and electrolytes are monitored. The nurse reviews renal function and effluent rate, coordinates drug level timing, and communicates the change in CRRT status to the prescriber and pharmacist so that doses are matched to the patient's current clearance. Because drug accumulation or underdosing each carry risk, an individualized plan supported by monitoring and interdisciplinary communication is essential during this dynamic therapy."},
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"High-Risk Drugs for Nephrotoxicity","body":"Certain medications can injure the kidney and must be used with caution, especially in patients with preexisting kidney disease, older adults, or the volume-depleted. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) reduce renal blood flow by inhibiting prostaglandins and can cause acute kidney injury, especially in combination with diuretics and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, a combination sometimes called the triple whammy. Aminoglycosides, amphotericin B, and certain chemotherapy agents are directly nephrotoxic, and iodinated contrast can cause contrast-induced nephropathy, mitigated with adequate hydration and consideration of iso-osmolar contrast. Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers dilate the efferent arteriole; they are renal-protective in many settings but can acutely raise creatinine or cause hyperkalemia when volume is depleted or renal artery stenosis is present. Proton pump inhibitors, when used long term, are associated with acute interstitial nephritis. Loop and thiazide diuretics can precipitate volume depletion and electrolyte disturbances, while lithium and calcineurin inhibitors like tacrolimus have nephrotoxic potential that requires therapeutic monitoring. When a nephrotoxic drug is necessary, the nurse minimizes risk by assessing baseline renal function, ensuring euvolemia, avoiding concurrent combinations that increase injury, monitoring creatinine and urine output, and promptly reporting a rise in creatinine or a decrease in urine output. The patient is educated to avoid over-the-counter NSAIDs and herbal products that harm the kidney, to maintain hydration, and to report dark urine, swelling, or reduced urine output. In patients with chronic kidney disease, the dose and duration of nephrotoxic drugs are minimized, and alternatives are chosen whenever possible. Coordination with the prescriber and pharmacist, plus vigilant monitoring, is central to preventing drug-induced kidney injury."},
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"Managing Drug Interactions With Dialysis and Immunosuppressants","body":"Patients on renal replacement therapy and immunosuppression face a high burden of drug interactions that require careful management. Immunosuppressants such as tacrolimus, cyclosporine, and mycophenolate have narrow therapeutic windows and interact with many medications, including antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, calcium channel blockers, and antiepileptics, which can raise or lower their levels and precipitate rejection or toxicity. Calcineurin inhibitors are metabolized by the CYP3A4 system; inhibitors like ketoconazole and clarithromycin raise levels, while inducers like rifampin and phenytoin lower levels, so drug levels are monitored and doses adjusted when interacting agents are introduced or stopped. Grapefruit juice inhibits CYP3A4 and should be avoided with calcineurin inhibitors. Mycophenolate interacts with drugs affecting enterohepatic recirculation and with antacids that reduce absorption. The dialysis patient may also take multiple agents for hypertension, anemia, bone disease, infection, and comorbid conditions, each with potential interactions, and the clearance of drugs removed by dialysis affects both efficacy and dosing. The patient who is volume depleted on diuretics is more vulnerable to injury with nephrotoxic drugs, and combinations that increase potassium, such as angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors with potassium-sparing diuretics or potassium supplements, require monitoring. The nurse obtains a complete and current medication list, including over-the-counter drugs and supplements, screens for known interacting combinations, checks serum drug levels for narrow-therapeutic-index agents, and educates the patient to report new symptoms and to avoid grapefruit and unapproved supplements. Coordination with the pharmacist is essential to reconcile doses whenever a new medication is started or stopped, when renal function changes, or when the dialysis prescription changes. Monitoring of drug levels, renal function, electrolytes, and clinical response, with a plan for adjusting doses, keeps the regimen effective and safe in a population that is particularly vulnerable to both rejection and drug-related toxicity."},
  {"topic":"Drug Dosing in Renal Failure","title":"Clinical Monitoring of Drug Therapy in Renal Failure","body":"Safe drug therapy in renal failure relies on systematic clinical and laboratory monitoring rather than a single dose calculation. The nurse assesses renal function, estimated by serum creatinine and estimated glomerular filtration rate, before the first dose and regularly thereafter, because renal function changes with disease progression and acute illness. For drugs with a narrow therapeutic index, serum drug concentrations are measured at the appropriate times, and the results guide dose adjustment; examples include vancomycin, aminoglycosides, digoxin, lithium, and the calcineurin inhibitors. Electrolytes, especially serum potassium, calcium, and phosphate, are monitored because many drugs affect them or because renal failure disturbs them. Volume status, reflected in daily weights, intake and output, blood pressure, and edema, is assessed because both hypovolemia and hypervolemia affect drug handling and the risk of nephrotoxicity. Signs of drug toxicity—such as confusion, tremor, ototoxicity from aminoglycosides, or a rising creatinine indicating nephrotoxicity—are reported promptly. The nurse documents the renal estimate used, the dosing basis, the timing of drug levels relative to dialysis, and any adjustments made, and communicates with the prescriber and pharmacist when changes occur. Patient education focuses on the reason for monitoring, expected side effects, the importance of taking doses at the correct time relative to dialysis, and the need to report symptoms. The team reassesses the regimen whenever the patient's renal function changes, when a new medication is added or stopped, when the dialysis prescription changes, or when the patient begins or stops an interacting drug. In acute kidney injury the dose is often adjusted downward and re-evaluated frequently because renal function can change quickly. Ultimately, individualized dosing supported by monitoring, documentation, and communication optimizes effectiveness and minimizes toxicity in the renal patient."},
  // ================= ACID-BASE DISORDERS (add 9) =================
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"The Arterial Blood Gas and Simple Interpretation","body":"An arterial blood gas is the standard way to assess acid-base status and consists of pH, partial pressure of carbon dioxide, partial pressure of oxygen, and serum bicarbonate. The pH reflects the overall acid-base balance, with the normal range roughly from 7.35 to 7.45; a pH below 7.35 is acidemia and above 7.45 is alkalemia. The primary disorder is then identified by the respiratory and metabolic components. The partial pressure of carbon dioxide is the respiratory component; an elevated PCO2 with acidemia indicates respiratory acidosis, and a low PCO2 with alkalemia indicates respiratory alkalosis. The bicarbonate is the metabolic component, regulated through the kidneys; a low bicarbonate with acidemia indicates metabolic acidosis, and a high bicarbonate with alkalemia suggests metabolic alkalosis. The pH change points to the primary disorder, because the compensatory response works to return the pH toward normal but rarely fully corrects it. A simple, systematic approach is to check the pH for acidemia or alkalemia, then determine whether the primary disturbance is respiratory or metabolic based on the PCO2 and bicarbonate, and finally consider whether appropriate compensation is present. Normal values vary slightly by source, but a useful framework is pH 7.35 to 7.45, PCO2 35 to 45 mmHg, and bicarbonate 22 to 26 mEq/L. In a patient with metabolic acidosis, the lungs compensate by hyperventilating to lower PCO2; in respiratory acidosis, the kidneys retain bicarbonate to compensate, though this takes time. The nurse gathers the blood gas with a properly heparinized sample and documents the inspired oxygen and the patient's clinical condition, because the clinical context, including renal function and volume status, is essential to interpret the values correctly. Serial gases and trend analysis are more useful than a single value. Understanding the normal values, the meaning of each component, and the compensation pattern allows the clinician to identify the primary disorder, gauge its severity, and guide therapy."},
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"Anion Gap and the Approach to Metabolic Acidosis","body":"The serum anion gap is a helpful first step in evaluating metabolic acidosis because it separates disorders in which unmeasured anions accumulate from those where hyperchloremia and bicarbonate loss predominate. The anion gap is calculated as serum sodium minus the sum of serum chloride and serum bicarbonate. The normal is usually around 8 to 12 mEq/L, though it may be a bit lower with modern analyzers; a value above 12 to 16 suggests the presence of unmeasured anions. A high anion gap metabolic acidosis is caused by the accumulation of organic acids, and the common causes are remembered by the mnemonic MUDPILES: methanol, uremia, diabetic ketoacidosis, propylene glycol, iron or isoniazid, lactic acidosis, ethylene glycol, and salicylates. A normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, by contrast, is usually due to loss of bicarbonate with retention of chloride, and the common causes include diarrhea, renal tubular acidosis, and acetazolamide or other carbonic anhydrase inhibitors; in these states chloride is retained to maintain electroneutrality, so the anion gap stays normal. When approaching a metabolic acidosis, the first step is to confirm the pH and low bicarbonate, then calculate the anion gap. If the gap is elevated, the clinician evaluates for the listed organic acid causes, while a normal gap points toward a loss or retention disorder. The urine anion gap and the osmolal gap can be helpful in specific cases, for example the urine anion gap in type 1 renal tubular acidosis, and the osmolal gap in methanol or ethylene glycol poisoning. The diagnosis is guided by the clinical picture, the history of possible ingestions, the serum glucose, lactate, ketones, and kidney function. The nurse obtains the relevant serum electrolytes and blood gas, calculates the anion gap, and reports the results with the clinical context, because the anion gap helps direct the workup and treatment. Monitoring serial values is important, and therapy targeting the specific cause, whether insulin for ketoacidosis, fluid and dialysis for poisoning, or bicarbonate for certain losses, is more effective than treating the number alone."},
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"Respiratory Acidosis and Its Management","body":"Respiratory acidosis results from inadequate ventilation leading to accumulation of carbon dioxide, which drives the pH down. The primary causes are a fall in alveolar ventilation due to hypoventilation from central nervous system depression, neuromuscular weakness, airway obstruction, chest wall restriction, or disorders of the lungs such as severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, pneumonia, and acute respiratory distress syndrome. Acute respiratory acidosis develops rapidly when the patient cannot blow off carbon dioxide, while chronic respiratory acidosis is seen in longstanding lung disease where the kidneys have compensated by retaining bicarbonate. The arterial blood gas shows a low pH and an elevated PCO2 with an elevated bicarbonate in the chronic state. The clinical signs reflect respiratory failure and hypercapnia, including drowsiness, confusion, headache, and, in severe cases, somnolence and respiratory depression. The treatment focuses on improving ventilation, not on giving bicarbonate, because the underlying problem is carbon dioxide retention. The nurse assists with positioning, bronchodilators, oxygen as indicated, and noninvasive positive pressure ventilation or mechanical ventilation in the patient who is failing, and monitors respiratory rate, effort, oxygen saturation, and level of consciousness. In chronic respiratory failure, the goal is to correct the gas exchange gradually and avoid excessive oxygen that suppresses the respiratory drive in the chronic hypercapnic patient, though oxygen is always given when the patient is hypoxic, with careful monitoring. Complications include the effects of respiratory muscle fatigue, carbon dioxide narcosis, and, in ventilated patients, the risk of ventilator-associated lung injury if tidal volume or pressure is too high. The nurse educates the patient with chronic lung disease about smoking cessation, pulmonary rehabilitation, and recognition of worsening symptoms, and coordinates with respiratory therapy. Serial blood gases and clinical trends guide adjustments in support. The prognosis depends on the reversibility of the underlying process; prompt recognition of hypoventilation and effective ventilation are the cornerstones of management."},
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"Respiratory Alkalosis and Its Management","body":"Respiratory alkalosis results from a decrease in the partial pressure of carbon dioxide due to hyperventilation, which raises the pH. The most common causes are anxiety and panic, high altitude, fever, pain, and conditions that stimulate the respiratory drive such as sepsis, hepatic failure, pregnancy, salicylate toxicity, and pulmonary embolism. The blood gas shows an elevated pH and a low PCO2, and in the acute setting the bicarbonate falls only modestly, while chronic respiratory alkalosis is accompanied by renal compensation with loss of bicarbonate. The clinical manifestations reflect hyperventilation and a decreased ionized calcium, which can cause perioral numbness, tingling in the hands and feet, muscle cramps, and in severe cases tetany and carpal-pedal spasm. The key to management is to identify and treat the underlying cause rather than simply trying to slow breathing, because the hyperventilation is often a physiologic response or a symptom of a serious condition such as sepsis or pulmonary embolism. For the anxious patient with simple hyperventilation, rebreathing techniques such as breathing into a paper bag or cupped hands can raise carbon dioxide and relieve symptoms, and reassurance and breathing retraining are helpful, but anxiety-driven hyperventilation is a diagnosis of exclusion after other causes are ruled out. If the cause is a metabolic stimulus such as salicylate toxicity, fever, or hypoxia, addressing that stimulus resolves the alkalosis. The nurse measures respiratory rate and depth, assesses oxygenation with pulse oximetry and arterial blood gas, screens for the provocative clinical picture, and monitors serum electrolytes and ionized calcium while symptoms are present. In salicylate toxicity, respiratory alkalosis is an early feature and may be followed by metabolic acidosis as the poisoning progresses, so serial gases are important. Management focuses on the underlying condition, symptom relief, and monitoring, and when an identifiable cause such as hypoxia or sepsis is treated, the alkalosis corrects as ventilation normalizes."},
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"Metabolic Alkalosis and Its Management","body":"Metabolic alkalosis is characterized by a high serum bicarbonate and an elevated pH, occurring when bicarbonate is gained or hydrogen ions are lost. The most common causes are loss of hydrogen ion from the gastrointestinal tract due to vomiting or gastric suction, and loss of hydrogen ion by the kidney due to diuretic therapy, as well as hypokalemia, hyperaldosteronism, and conditions that increase the renal reabsorption of bicarbonate. Chloride-responsive alkalosis, seen with vomiting and diuretics, is corrected by administering chloride, often as saline, because the alkalosis is associated with volume and chloride depletion. Chloride-resistant alkalosis, seen with hyperaldosteronism and severe hypokalemia, does not correct with saline alone and requires addressing the underlying condition and potassium deficit. The blood gas shows an elevated pH, elevated bicarbonate, and, in a compensatory response, an elevated PCO2 as the lungs retain carbon dioxide. The clinical features include weakness, muscle cramps, hypokalemia with associated electrocardiogram changes, and, because alkalemia lowers ionized calcium, possible tetany and seizures. Treatment is directed at the cause and at restoring volume, chloride, and potassium; saline administration expands volume and allows the kidney to excrete bicarbonate, while potassium replacement is essential because hypokalemia both contributes to and perpetuates the alkalosis. The nurse monitors serum electrolytes, especially potassium and chloride, urine output, and volume status, and reports electrocardiogram changes, and administers potassium safely with attention to the concentration and the site of infusion. The patient is educated about the risk of losing stomach acid with prolonged vomiting, the proper use of diuretics, and the importance of replacing potassium as directed. Because metabolic alkalosis can be sustained by continued volume or chloride depletion, careful assessment and correction of volume and electrolytes are the foundation of therapy, and serial blood gases are used to confirm resolution."},
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"Renal Tubular Acidosis Types and Diagnosis","body":"Renal tubular acidosis refers to a group of disorders in which the kidneys fail to maintain normal acid-base balance despite a normal glomerular filtration rate, because of defective tubular handling of bicarbonate or hydrogen ion. Type 1, distal renal tubular acidosis, results from inability of the distal nephron to excrete hydrogen ion, causing a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with urine that fails to acidify, and it is associated with nephrocalcinosis and kidney stones. Type 2, proximal renal tubular acidosis, results from impaired proximal tubular bicarbonate reabsorption, causing a bicarbonate-wasting state that may be accompanied by other proximal tubular defects, and it can occur in multiple myeloma, in certain drugs, and in inherited syndromes. Type 4 is caused by aldosterone deficiency or resistance and is most often seen in diabetic nephropathy, interstitial nephritis, and obstruction, characterized by mild hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis. The diagnosis involves confirming a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis and then differentiating the type based on the urine pH, the urine anion gap, the presence or absence of hyperkalemia, and the response to bicarbonate loading. The history and clinical features, including a history of stones, nephrocalcinosis, or autoimmune disease, guide the workup. Treatment varies by type: type 1 responds to alkali therapy, often sodium bicarbonate or citrate, and to correction of associated hypokalemia, and control of the acidosis protects against stones and bone disease; type 2 may require large amounts of alkali and treatment of the underlying cause; type 4 is managed by reducing potassium, possibly with a mineralocorticoid or other agents, and treating hyperkalemia. The nephrology nurse monitors serum electrolytes, urine pH, and response to therapy, educates the patient about the importance of taking prescribed alkali and following blood and urine monitoring, and recognizes the complications of chronic acidosis such as bone demineralization, growth failure in children, and nephrolithiasis. Multidisciplinary follow-up and adherence to therapy are essential to prevent the long-term complications of chronic metabolic acidosis."},
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"Bicarbonate Therapy and Dialysis in Acidosis","body":"Bicarbonate therapy is used in metabolic acidosis when the pH falls to dangerous levels or when specific conditions call for it, but its routine use is reserved because there may be limited benefit and potential harm in some settings. Sodium bicarbonate can raise the pH and helps in severe acidemia, hyperkalemia, and certain poisonings such as salicylate and tricyclic antidepressant toxicity, where alkalinization improves drug elimination and reduces tissue toxicity. In lactic acidosis and cardiac arrest, empiric high-dose bicarbonate is generally avoided because it may shift the oxyhemoglobin curve, increase carbon dioxide production, cause volume and sodium overload, and worsen intracellular acidosis, unless there is an indication such as severe hyperkalemia. When bicarbonate is given, it is infused slowly and monitored carefully because rapid correction can cause hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, and volume overload, and it can generate carbon dioxide that must be ventilated. In renal failure and severe uremic acidosis, dialysis is the definitive therapy, because it removes acid, waste products, and potassium while delivering buffer. The choice of dialysate buffer, including bicarbonate, is standard in modern hemodialysis, and in continuous renal replacement therapy a bicarbonate-based buffer is commonly used and preferred for critically ill patients. Dialysis is indicated when acidosis is refractory to medical management, when hyperkalemia or fluid overload are severe, or when uremic complications develop. The nurse monitors the pH, serum bicarbonate, potassium, calcium, and volume status during therapy, ensures adequate ventilation if the patient is receiving bicarbonate, and documents the response. The patient with chronic kidney disease and metabolic acidosis is educated about dietary protein and the use of oral alkali when prescribed, because chronic acidosis contributes to bone disease and progression of kidney injury. Ultimately, treating the underlying cause, maintaining an adequate buffer, and using dialysis when indicated restore acid-base balance safely."},
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"Mixed Acid-Base Disorders and Compensation","body":"Mixed acid-base disorders occur when more than one primary disturbance is present simultaneously, and recognizing them requires checking whether the measured compensation matches what would be expected for a single disorder. In a simple disorder, the compensatory response follows predictable relationships, so the degree to which the PCO2 or bicarbonate changes in response can be estimated. For example, in acute respiratory acidosis, the bicarbonate rises modestly, while in chronic respiratory acidosis the bicarbonate is higher; in metabolic acidosis, the expected fall in PCO2 is about 1.2 to 1.5 times the fall in bicarbonate, and in metabolic alkalosis the PCO2 rises accordingly. If the compensation is more or less than predicted, a second disorder is present. A patient with metabolic acidosis and respiratory acidosis, for instance, has a lower pH than expected for the metabolic acidemia alone, while a patient with metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis has a nearer-normal or even high pH despite a low bicarbonate. Clinical examples include diabetic ketoacidosis with vomiting, which superimposes metabolic alkalosis, or a patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and acute metabolic acidosis. The approach is to calculate the anion gap, assess the primary pH and the components, then compare the measured compensation to the predicted compensation using standard formulas, and consider the clinical history for processes that would add a second disturbance. The nurse gathers serial blood gases, serum electrolytes, and the clinical picture, and recognizes that laboratory error, such as a venous sample or improper heparin, can mislead interpretation, so the values are assessed in context and sometimes repeated. Management addresses each primary disturbance, monitoring the pH and clinical status as therapy is adjusted. Understanding expected compensation is a key skill because it reveals the mixed nature of many acid-base problems and directs treatment to both the metabolic and respiratory components."},
  // ================= FLUID AND ELECTROLYTE EMERGENCIES (add 9) =================
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Initial Assessment of the Patient With Electrolyte Disturbance","body":"The initial assessment of a patient with an electrolyte disturbance begins with the airway, breathing, and circulation, because severe electrolyte abnormalities can cause life-threatening arrhythmias, respiratory failure, and altered mental status. The nurse immediately obtains vital signs and rhythm monitoring, because the electrocardiogram may show characteristic changes of hyperkalemia or hypokalemia, and because cardiac arrest is the most feared complication. A focused history identifies risk factors such as chronic kidney disease, heart failure, diabetes, diuretic use, vomiting, diarrhea, and recent dialysis, which narrow the likely disturbance. A complete blood count and a comprehensive metabolic panel, including sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate, calcium, magnesium, and phosphorus, are obtained, and renal function is assessed. The physical examination looks for signs of volume depletion or overload, edema, muscle weakness, tetany, and neurologic changes; the presence of muscle cramps, paresthesias, or altered mental status gives clues to specific imbalances. The severity of the disturbance, the acuity of its onset, and the patient's overall condition determine whether it must be treated emergently. For example, a very high potassium, a severely low or high sodium, or a marked calcium disturbance with symptoms requires prompt intervention, while milder asymptomatic abnormalities may be corrected gradually. The nurse ensures that the patient is on a cardiac monitor, has intravenous access, and that emergency equipment and agents such as calcium, insulin, bicarbonate, and potassium are available. Serial laboratory testing and electrocardiograms guide therapy, because electrolyte values can shift quickly and treatment responses vary. The nurse reports the results with the clinical context, coordinates with the provider, and rechecks levels after interventions. Because iatrogenic errors, such as rapid correction of hyponatremia or excessive potassium replacement, are dangerous, the plan emphasizes slow, monitored correction based on symptoms and severity. Early, systematic assessment and close monitoring are the keys to preventing the most serious outcomes."},
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Hyperkalemia: Electrocardiogram Changes and Emergency Management","body":"Hyperkalemia is a life-threatening emergency because elevated potassium depolarizes the myocardial cell membranes and can cause ventricular arrhythmia and cardiac arrest, with the electrocardiogram providing an early warning. As potassium rises, characteristic electrocardiogram changes progress: peaking or tall, narrow T waves appear first, then the PR interval widens, the P wave flattens and disappears, the QRS complex widens, and ultimately a sine wave pattern leads to asystole or ventricular fibrillation. The ECG should be obtained immediately and repeated frequently, because the degree of potassium correlates with the risk and the changes can progress rapidly. Emergency management is staged and is directed toward stabilizing the myocardium, shifting potassium into cells, and removing potassium from the body. Intravenous calcium, usually calcium gluconate or calcium chloride, stabilizes the myocardial membrane and works within minutes, protecting against arrhythmia while the potassium is lowered by other means; it does not lower the serum potassium itself. Insulin with glucose shifts potassium into cells within about 15 to 30 minutes, and inhaled beta-agonists such as albuterol also drive potassium intracellularly. Sodium bicarbonate can help, particularly in acidosis, though its benefit is less certain. To remove potassium, loop diuretics can increase renal excretion in patients with residual kidney function, and cation-exchange resins such as patiromer or sodium zirconium cyclosilicate can bind potassium in the gut; Kayexalate is generally avoided because of its association with colonic necrosis. Dialysis, particularly hemodialysis, is the definitive removal for severe or refractory hyperkalemia, especially in renal failure. The nurse prepares and administers the medications, monitors the cardiac rhythm, and draws serial potassium levels. The patient is placed on a monitor, intravenous access is confirmed, and calcium is given slowly with a check of the heart because it can cause bradycardia. After the acute episode, the cause is addressed, dietary potassium is reviewed, and medications that raise potassium are adjusted. Rapid, coordinated, staged treatment is essential to prevent fatal arrhythmia."},
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Hypokalemia: Causes, Electrocardiogram Changes, and Treatment","body":"Hypokalemia refers to a low serum potassium and can cause serious cardiac and neuromuscular complications. The most common causes are losses from the gastrointestinal tract with vomiting, diarrhea, or nasogastric suction and losses from the kidney due to diuretics, particularly loop and thiazide diuretics, as well as hyperaldosteronism and magnesium deficiency. A shift of potassium into cells, as occurs with insulin therapy, beta-agonists, and alkalosis, can also cause hypokalemia without total body depletion. The electrocardiogram typically shows flattened T waves, ST-segment depression, and a prominent U wave, and as the potassium falls further, the risk of atrial and ventricular arrhythmias rises, including torsades de pointes, especially in patients taking digoxin. Symptoms include muscle weakness, cramps, fatigue, constipation, and in severe cases paralysis and rhabdomyolysis. Treatment replaces potassium, giving it preferably by mouth for mild deficits, and by intravenous route for severe, symptomatic, or refractory hypokalemia, with the dose, concentration, and rate guided by the severity and by central or peripheral access. Potassium is never given by rapid bolus because it can cause fatal hyperkalemia and arrhythmia, and it must be administered with caution in patients with renal impairment, in whom excretion is reduced. Magnesium is replaced when low, because hypomagnesemia makes hypokalemia difficult to correct. The nurse monitors serum potassium frequently, watches the cardiac rhythm and vital signs, and observes the intravenous site for phlebitis and for signs of tissue damage if extravasation occurs. The underlying cause is identified and treated; for example, the diuretic may be reduced, and potassium-sparing measures considered. The patient is educated about potassium-rich foods and the proper use of supplements, and about symptoms of hypokalemia and its treatment. Careful, monitored replacement guided by serial levels and clinical status prevents both the complications of low potassium and the dangers of overcorrection."},
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Hyponatremia and the Safe Correction of Serum Sodium","body":"Hyponatremia, a serum sodium below about 135 mEq/L, is the most common electrolyte disorder and can cause cerebral edema when it develops rapidly or is corrected too quickly. The symptoms range from headache, nausea, and confusion to seizures and coma as the sodium falls, because water shifts into brain cells and causes swelling. Management depends on the severity, the acuity of the onset, whether symptoms are present, and the patient's volume status. In severe, symptomatic hyponatremia, hypertonic saline is used to raise the sodium rapidly enough to reduce cerebral edema and prevent brain injury, but the correction must be limited to a safe rate. Overcorrection, raising sodium too quickly, can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome, a serious and often irreversible neurologic injury, so the total rise in the first 24 hours is generally kept below about 8 to 10 mEq/L, and slower when risk factors for demyelination are present. In chronic or asymptomatic hyponatremia, the correction is gradual, and the cause is addressed, whether it is volume overload with dilution, volume depletion with sodium loss, or the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone, in which free water is retained. Fluid restriction is often the cornerstone for euvolemic hyponatremia, and saline is used in volume depletion, while addressing the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone may involve water restriction, loop diuretics, and sometimes vasopressin antagonists. The nurse monitors serum sodium frequently, checks neurologic status, tracks intake and output and weight, and ensures that the sodium is not corrected too rapidly; frequent sampling and a documented correction plan are essential. The patient is educated about fluid restriction and the importance of reporting headache, confusion, or weakness. Because the rapid correction of hyponatremia carries significant risk, the guiding principle is to correct symptoms at a safe, monitored rate and to treat the underlying cause."},
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Hypernatremia and Safe Correction of Serum Sodium","body":"Hypernatremia, a serum sodium above about 145 mEq/L, generally reflects a deficit of free water relative to sodium in the body, because the normal sensation of thirst and the renal conserving mechanisms protect against it unless the patient cannot drink, loses water excessively, or receives hypertonic solutions. The most common causes are inadequate water intake, especially in the elderly, infants, or those with altered mental status; excessive water loss from fever, sweating, diarrhea, or diabetes insipidus; and sometimes sodium gain. Symptoms, which arise primarily from brain cell shrinkage as water shifts out of cells, include weakness, irritability, confusion, and in severe cases seizures, coma, and cerebral hemorrhage. The treatment is to replace the free water deficit and correct the cause, but the sodium must be lowered gradually, generally by no more than about 8 to 10 mEq/L per 24 hours, because rapid correction causes water to shift into brain cells and can precipitate cerebral edema and seizures. The choice of replacement fluid and the rate depend on the volume status; the volume-depleted patient receives isotonic fluid to restore circulation first, while the euvolemic or hypervolemic patient receives free water by mouth or hypotonic fluid. The nurse monitors the serum sodium frequently, assesses neurologic status, tracks intake and output and weight, and calculates the rate of correction so that the sodium does not fall too quickly. In diabetes insipidus, the treatment includes addressing the lack of antidiuretic hormone or renal response, sometimes with desmopressin, and ensuring that the patient's intake matches losses at all times. The patient is educated about water intake, the signs of dehydration, and the importance of reporting confusion or altered mental status. Because both the high sodium and its rapid correction are dangerous, slow, monitored replacement guided by serial levels is the standard of care."},
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Hypocalcemia and the Recognition of Tetany","body":"Hypocalcemia, a low serum calcium, increases neuromuscular excitability and can cause the classic signs of tetany, including perioral numbness, tingling in the hands and feet, muscle cramps, and in severe cases carpal-pedal spasm and laryngospasm. The Trousseau sign, in which carpal spasm is elicited by inflating a blood pressure cuff, and the Chvostek sign, in which tapping the facial nerve causes twitching, are physical findings of latent tetany. Severe hypocalcemia can also cause seizures and a prolonged QT interval with the risk of arrhythmia. The common causes include chronic kidney disease with hypocalcemia and secondary hyperparathyroidism, hypoparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, magnesium deficiency, pancreatitis, sepsis, and massive blood transfusion. Because much of the calcium is protein-bound, the ionized calcium is the physiologically active fraction and should be measured directly in conditions that alter protein or pH. Treatment of symptomatic hypocalcemia includes intravenous calcium gluconate or calcium chloride given slowly with cardiac monitoring, because rapid administration can cause arrhythmia, and correction of any concurrent magnesium deficiency, since hypocalcemia is difficult to correct if magnesium is low. Oral calcium and vitamin D are used for maintenance and for chronic management, particularly in renal failure. The nurse watches the electrocardiogram during calcium infusion, monitors for calcium extravasation, and checks serum calcium, magnesium, and phosphate. The patient is educated about the signs of hypocalcemia, the importance of calcium and vitamin D supplementation, and the need to report tetany or paresthesias. In renal osteodystrophy, the calcium, phosphate, and parathyroid hormone axis is managed together, so monitoring all the related values and the response to therapy is essential. Prompt recognition of tetany and safe correction prevent the complications of severe hypocalcemia."},
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Hypercalcemia and Its Acute Management","body":"Hypercalcemia, a high serum calcium, can cause progressive symptoms and is an emergency when severe or when neurologic or cardiovascular effects appear. The symptoms range from fatigue, weakness, constipation, and polyuria to confusion, lethargy, and coma, with cardiac manifestations including a shortened QT interval and a risk of arrhythmia. The common causes include primary hyperparathyroidism, malignancy, and, in the renal patient, calcium-based phosphate binders and oversuppression of parathyroid hormone, as well as vitamin D excess and immobilization. Initial management focuses on the severity of the hypercalcemia and the presence of symptoms. Expansion of the intravascular volume with intravenous saline increases renal calcium excretion and is often the first step, and loop diuretics, once the patient is volume replete, enhance calcium excretion, though they carry a risk of hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia. For more resistant hypercalcemia, calcitonin lowers calcium quickly but temporarily, and bisphosphonates such as zoledronic acid act more slowly and are used in malignancy-associated hypercalcemia to inhibit bone resorption. Denosumab is an alternative in some cases, and glucocorticoids are helpful in conditions where they reduce calcium, particularly certain cancers and sarcoidosis. In severe refractory hypercalcemia with renal failure, dialysis with a low-calcium dialysate can remove calcium directly. The nurse monitors the serum calcium, electrolytes, vital signs, volume status, and the electrocardiogram, and administers hydration while watching for fluid overload, which is a risk in patients with cardiac or renal disease. The patient is educated about symptoms of high calcium, the importance of hydration, and the need to stop calcium-based supplements if appropriate. The underlying cause, whether parathyroid disease, malignancy, or medication, is identified and treated. Prompt recognition and stepwise therapy, moving from hydration to pharmacologic agents and dialysis as needed, are the keys to managing acute hypercalcemia."},
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Magnesium Disturbances in the Renal Patient","body":"Magnesium balance is frequently disturbed in renal disease, and both hyponatremia and hypermagnesemia require attention because magnesium affects neuromuscular and cardiac function. Hypermagnesemia occurs when renal excretion is impaired, and it can develop in patients with renal failure, particularly those taking magnesium-containing laxatives or antacids, or with aggressive replacement. It causes weakness, bradycardia, hypotension, depressed deep tendon reflexes, and at very high levels respiratory depression and cardiac arrest. Treatment includes stopping magnesium intake, giving calcium intravenously to antagonize its effects acutely, using loop diuretics to enhance excretion when renal function allows, and in severe cases dialysis, which removes magnesium effectively. Hypermagnesemia may accompany hypercalcemia and can cause electrocardiogram changes such as a prolonged PR interval, widening of the QRS, and a prolonged QT interval. Hypomagnesemia is common from gastrointestinal losses, diuretic use, alcoholism, and loop diuretics, and it predisposes to hypokalemia and hypocalcemia that are difficult to correct until magnesium is replaced. Symptoms include muscle cramping, tremor, and seizures, and the electrocardiogram may show QT prolongation and a risk of torsades de pointes. Treatment replaces magnesium, orally for mild deficiency, and intravenously for severe or symptomatic cases, with monitoring of the serum magnesium, reflexes, and vital signs, because rapid or excessive magnesium can cause hypotension and respiratory depression. The nurse monitors serum magnesium, calcium, and potassium together, because the ions interact, and observes the patient for tetany, weakness, and electrocardiogram changes. In renal failure, magnesium is given cautiously because clearance is reduced, and magnesium-containing medications are used with care. Identifying the cause of the disturbance and replacing or removing the mineral at a monitored rate prevents the cardiac and neuromuscular complications of abnormal magnesium balance."},
  // ================= TRANSPLANT PHARMACOLOGY (add 9) =================
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"Principles of Immunosuppression in Kidney Transplant","body":"Immunosuppression after kidney transplant aims to prevent rejection while limiting the risk of infection, malignancy, and other drug toxicities, and it is built on a balance between suppressing the immune system enough to protect the graft and preserving enough immunity to defend the host. The regimen typically has three layers: induction, maintenance, and treatment of rejection. Induction therapy is given at or around the time of transplant to provide potent early prophylaxis and may use a biologic agent that depletes lymphocytes, such as a thymoglobulin or an anti-CD52 agent, or a non-depleting antibody that blocks lymphocyte activation, such as an IL-2 receptor antagonist. Maintenance immunosuppression is used long term to prevent rejection and usually combines drugs with different mechanisms, most commonly a calcineurin inhibitor such as tacrolimus or cyclosporine, an antiproliferative agent such as mycophenolate, and a corticosteroid, though steroid-sparing regimens are increasingly used. Treatment of rejection uses higher-dose corticosteroids, and for antibody-mediated or resistant rejection, additional agents such as intravenous immunoglobulin, plasmapheresis, and antibody treatments. The nurse plays a central role in teaching the patient about the purpose, dose, schedule, side effects, and interactions of each medication, emphasizing the lifelong and life-critical nature of adherence, because nonadherence is a major cause of late graft loss. Drug levels are measured for agents with a narrow therapeutic index, such as tacrolimus and cyclosporine, and the dose is adjusted to maintain target ranges, avoiding both underimmunosuppression, which risks rejection, and overimmunosuppression, which risks infection and malignancy. The nurse monitors for infections, especially opportunistic organisms, and for complications such as hypertension, diabetes, nephrotoxicity, and malignancy. The patient is taught to recognize signs of rejection, such as a rise in creatinine, fluid retention, or decreased urine output, and the importance of reporting them promptly. Immunosuppression is individualized based on the recipient's immunologic risk, the donor type, and the patient's other conditions, and it is adjusted over time. Lifelong follow-up, coordination, and education are essential to maintaining graft function."},
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"Calcineurin Inhibitors and Their Monitoring","body":"Calcineurin inhibitors, including tacrolimus and cyclosporine, are central to maintenance immunosuppression in kidney transplant, and they act by inhibiting the calcineurin pathway that drives T-cell activation. Because they have a narrow therapeutic index and significant toxicity, their serum levels are measured and the dose is adjusted to a target range that balances rejection prevention against harm. Nephrotoxicity is the most important and dose-limiting side effect, occurring acutely as a rise in serum creatinine from afferent arteriolar constriction and chronically from interstitial fibrosis, and it can be difficult to distinguish from rejection, so a kidney biopsy is sometimes needed. Other common effects include hypertension, hyperkalemia, tremor, and, for tacrolimus, hyperglycemia and new-onset diabetes; cyclosporine is associated with hirsutism, gingival hyperplasia, and hyperlipidemia. Both agents are metabolized by the cytochrome P450 system, predominantly CYP3A4, so many drugs and foods interact with them: inhibitors such as the azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, and grapefruit juice raise levels, while inducers such as rifampin and phenytoin lower them, so drug levels must be followed closely when interacting agents are started or stopped. The nurse measures levels at the correct time, usually a 12-hour trough for tacrolimus taken before the morning dose, and coordinates with the pharmacy and provider to adjust the dose. Monitoring includes serum creatinine, blood pressure, potassium, lipids, and blood glucose, along with the drug level, and the patient is educated about taking the medication at the same time each day, avoiding grapefruit, and reporting tremor, palpitations, or a rise in creatinine. Because overimmunosuppression increases the risk of infection and malignancy, and underexposure risks rejection, careful, coordinated drug monitoring with dose adjustment and patient education underlies the safe use of calcineurin inhibitors in transplantation."},
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"Antiproliferative Agents in Transplant","body":"Antiproliferative agents suppress the immune system by inhibiting the proliferation of activated lymphocytes and are a standard component of maintenance immunosuppression. Mycophenolate, available as mycophenolate mofetil or mycophenolic acid, inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase and is a key part of most regimens, typically paired with a calcineurin inhibitor and a steroid. Its main adverse effects are gastrointestinal, including diarrhea, nausea, and abdominal pain, and bone marrow suppression with leukopenia, anemia, and thrombocytopenia, so the complete blood count is monitored and the dose may be reduced if counts fall. Azathioprine is an older antiproliferative purine antagonist that is used sometimes, particularly when mycophenolate is not tolerated, and it can cause bone marrow suppression and liver dysfunction; the thiopurine methyltransferase enzyme activity is assessed before use because patients with low activity are at higher risk of severe marrow suppression. Mycophenolate is teratogenic and is contraindicated in pregnancy, so reproductive-aged patients are counseled about contraception and pregnancy planning. Drug interactions include reduced absorption with antacids and iron, and interference with the enterohepatic circulation, so the timing of administration and concurrent medications matters. The nurse monitors the blood count, especially leukocytes and platelets, reviews liver and kidney function, and teaches the patient to report infection, unusual bruising or bleeding, and gastrointestinal symptoms. Because antiproliferative agents contribute to the risk of infection and, with other immunosuppressants, malignancy, the patient is educated about infection precautions and cancer screening. The dose is often adjusted based on the white blood cell count, and the agent may be held or dose-reduced during infections or when counts are low. Adherence is reinforced because stopping these agents can precipitate rejection. Coordinated monitoring of blood counts, side effects, and interactions, along with patient education, is essential for the safe use of antiproliferative immunosuppression."},
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"mTOR Inhibitors: Sirolimus and Everolimus","body":"mTOR inhibitors, including sirolimus and everolimus, suppress the immune system by inhibiting the mammalian target of rapamycin pathway, which is involved in cell growth and proliferation, and they are used in kidney transplantation, sometimes in place of a calcineurin inhibitor to spare the kidney from nephrotoxicity, or as adjunctive therapy. They are associated with a distinctive set of adverse effects, especially delayed wound healing, which makes them generally avoided in the immediate postoperative period, and oral ulcers, hyperlipidemia, and peripheral edema. They can impair platelet function and are associated with proteinuria and an increase in blood pressure, and rarely with interstitial lung disease, presenting as cough and dyspnea. Because they are metabolized by the CYP3A4 pathway, they interact with the same classes of drugs and with grapefruit as the calcineurin inhibitors, and their levels are monitored to guide dosing and avoid toxicity. A well-known interaction is the increase in the levels and toxicity of mycophenolate and the potential for interactions with certain antibiotics and antifungals, so a careful medication review is necessary. The nurse monitors the drug level, serum lipids, proteinuria, blood count, and wound healing, and teaches the patient to report mouth ulcers, edema, cough, or difficulty breathing. Because of delayed wound healing, patients are advised about postoperative care and the timing of elective surgery. The diet is adjusted for hyperlipidemia and the effect of the drug. In the setting of a recent transplant, the timing of initiation and the combination with other agents are individualized. The patient is educated about the importance of taking the medication at the same time each day, reporting adverse effects early, and avoiding grapefruit and interacting drugs. Monitoring and education, along with coordination between the prescriber, pharmacist, and nurse, are essential to managing the benefits and risks of mTOR inhibitor therapy."},
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"Corticosteroids in the Transplant Patient","body":"Corticosteroids such as prednisone and methylprednisolone are a long-standing component of immunosuppression, used at high doses at the time of transplant and during treatment of rejection, and at lower maintenance doses in many patients, though steroid-sparing protocols are used in selected settings. Their beneficial effects include prevention and treatment of rejection, while their adverse effects are numerous and depend on the dose and duration: Cushingoid appearance, weight gain, hyperglycemia and new-onset diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, peptic ulcer disease, and an increased susceptibility to infection, along with mood changes and insomnia. The nurse monitors blood glucose, blood pressure, and the clinical response, and teaches the patient about measures to reduce the risks, including regular physical activity, a heart-healthy diet, blood sugar and blood pressure monitoring, calcium and vitamin D for bone health, and gastroprotection if a co-prescribed acid reducer is needed. Because steroids mask and lower resistance to infection, the patient is educated about infection precautions, and is warned never to stop corticosteroids abruptly, because rapid withdrawal can cause adrenal insufficiency, with symptoms such as fatigue, weakness, and hypotension. The dose is tapered gradually under medical supervision. Steroids also affect the risk of avascular necrosis of the hip, cataracts, and skin fragility, so the patient reports joint pain or visual changes. In the renal transplant patient, immunosuppression that includes steroids must be managed carefully with the other agents, balancing dose reductions when possible. The nurse reinforces adherence, coordinates diabetes and blood pressure management, and helps the patient understand the long-term implications of chronic steroid use. Education, monitoring, and gradual tapering are the keys to using corticosteroids effectively while limiting their many adverse effects."},
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"Induction Therapy and Depleting Agents","body":"Induction immunosuppression is given around the time of transplant to provide strong early protection against rejection, and it includes depleting agents that remove lymphocytes as well as non-depleting agents that block lymphocyte activation. Depleting induction, such as rabbit antithymocyte globulin, is a polyclonal antibody that binds to and depletes T cells; it is used in patients at higher immunologic risk and, because it profoundly suppresses the immune system, it increases the risk of infection and of a serious reaction during infusion, including fever, chills, and sometimes cytokine-release reactions, especially with the first dose, so premedication and careful observation are required. Alemtuzumab, an anti-CD52 agent, depletes both T and B cells and is another potent induction option. Non-depleting induction, such as an interleukin-2 receptor antagonist, blocks T-cell activation without depleting lymphocytes and is associated with fewer infectious complications, making it a choice for lower-risk or older recipients. The choice of induction depends on the recipient's sensitization, the donor type, the risk of rejection, and the risk of infection and malignancy. The nurse cares for the patient during induction, monitoring for infusion reactions, signs of infection, and cytopenias, and educates about the importance of infection precautions in the early post-transplant period. Because depleting agents predispose to opportunistic infections such as cytomegalovirus and to post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder, prophylaxis against these is often used and surveillance is carried out. The patient is monitored for a reduction in the white blood cell count and for any fever or signs of infection. Long-term, the effects of induction set the stage for the maintenance regimen, and the risk of malignancy persists. The nurse coordinates the premedication, the monitoring during infusion, and the ongoing surveillance for infection and cytopenias, and reinforces follow-up."},
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"Biologics and Antibodies in Rejection Therapy","body":"Antibody therapies are used to treat rejection, particularly antibody-mediated and steroid-resistant rejection, and they act by targeting specific cells or molecules. Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) neutralizes antibodies and modulates the immune response and is used in antibody-mediated rejection and to reduce sensitization; it can cause infusion reactions, volume overload, and renal dysfunction, and is given slowly with monitoring. Plasmapheresis removes circulating antibodies and is often combined with IVIG to deplete donor-specific antibody. Rituximab targets the CD20 molecule on B cells and is used in antibody-mediated rejection and to deplete B cells; it can cause infusion reactions, cytopenias, and an increased risk of infection. Agents that block complement, such as eculizumab, are used in severe or refractory antibody-mediated rejection to prevent complement-mediated injury, and because complement inhibition in the setting of meningococcal infection carries a particular risk, vaccination against meningococcus is required before use, along with prophylaxis in some settings. Rabbit antithymocyte globulin and alemtuzumab, described with induction, are also used to treat severe cellular rejection. The nurse administers these agents with attention to the rate and the monitoring required, watches for infusion reactions, fever, and signs of infection, monitors the blood count and renal function, and coordinates the timing relative to dialysis and other therapies. The patient is educated about the reason for the treatment, the expected course, the signs of infection, and the need for follow-up and, for women of childbearing age, contraception because some agents are teratogenic. Because these powerful therapies increase the risk of infection and malignancy, close surveillance and prophylaxis are essential. Effective treatment of rejection with biologics requires careful monitoring, patient education, and coordination across the transplant team."},
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"Infection Prophylaxis After Transplant","body":"Infection prophylaxis after transplant is essential because immunosuppression increases the risk of both common and opportunistic infections, and many are preventable with targeted therapy. Prophylaxis against cytomegalovirus and other herpes viruses is given for a defined period based on the donor and recipient serostatus and the intensity of immunosuppression; valganciclovir is commonly used and its dose is adjusted for renal function and monitored for marrow suppression. Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia is prevented with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, which also affords protection against some bacterial infections and urinary tract infections; for patients who cannot tolerate it, alternatives such as atovaquone, dapsone, or pentamidine are used, chosen according to sulfa allergy and other factors. Antifungal prophylaxis, often with nystatin or clotrimazole, reduces the risk of oral candidiasis, while systemic antifungal prophylaxis is used in selected high-risk patients because of interactions with immunosuppressive agents. In some recipients, prophylaxis against specific organisms, such as toxoplasmosis, is given. The duration of prophylaxis depends on the degree of immunosuppression and the risk profile, and the regimen is frequently reassessed. The nurse monitors the blood count, particularly for leukopenia, and drug levels for agents like valganciclovir, teaches the patient about the reason for prophylaxis, the importance of completing the course, and the signs of infection to report, including fever, cough, dysuria, or oral lesions, and reinforces good hand hygiene and avoidance of sick contacts. The nurse also ensures that the patient's vaccinations are up to date, understanding that live vaccines are generally avoided during significant immunosuppression. Because breakthrough infections can still occur and because infection is a leading cause of morbidity and graft loss, meticulous adherence to prophylaxis, monitoring, and education is central to the post-transplant care plan."},
  {"topic":"Transplant Pharmacology","title":"Medication Adherence and Its Impact on Graft Survival","body":"Medication adherence is one of the strongest determinants of long-term transplant graft survival, because nonadherence to immunosuppression is a leading cause of late acute rejection and graft loss. The consequences of missing doses are serious and often silent until renal function deteriorates, so the transplant team works to build habits, understanding, and support around the daily medication routine. The nurse assesses adherence openly and nonjudgmentally at each visit, explores barriers such as the cost of medications, complexity of the regimen, side effects, busy schedules, and forgetfulness, and uses simple tools such as pill counts, pharmacy refill records, and self-report questions. Adherence is promoted by simplifying the regimen when possible, aligning doses with daily routines, using pill organizers, setting alarms, and, in some patients, using automated reminders or monitoring devices. Education reinforces the purpose of each medication, the consequences of nonadherence, and the importance of never stopping immunosuppressants without the transplant team. Side effects, a major driver of nonadherence, are actively managed; if a drug is poorly tolerated, the team works with the patient and the pharmacist to adjust the dose or change the agent while maintaining adequate immunosuppression. The cost of medications is addressed through insurance navigation, generic options, and assistance programs. Psychosocial support, including mental health and financial counseling, is offered because depression and stress interfere with adherence. The nurse teaches the patient and caregivers to recognize signs of rejection, such as a rise in creatinine, decreased urine output, or swelling, and to report them immediately. Because adherence declines over time and after other health crises, sustained follow-up and repeated reinforcement are essential. A strong relationship built on trust and practical support keeps the graft healthy over the long term."},
  {"topic":"Acid-Base Disorders","title":"Lactic Acidosis and the Role of the Nurse","body":"Lactic acidosis is a high anion gap metabolic acidosis caused by an accumulation of lactate, and it commonly reflects tissue hypoxia or critical illness. The two major types are type A, due to inadequate tissue oxygenation from shock, sepsis, cardiac arrest, severe hypoxemia, or severe anemia, and type B, due to causes without overt hypoperfusion such as certain medications (metformin, linezolid, propofol), toxins (cyanide, ethanol), and inborn errors of metabolism. In the setting of shock, the lactate is a marker of the severity of oxygen debt, and serial lactate measurements are used to gauge the response to resuscitation. The blood gas shows a low pH, a low bicarbonate, and an elevated anion gap, and the serum lactate is elevated. The most important treatment is to correct the underlying cause, because restoring tissue perfusion is what clears lactate, and giving bicarbonate does not address the source. In metformin-associated lactic acidosis, which carries a high mortality, cessation of the drug, supportive care, hemodynamic support, and in severe cases dialysis, which removes both metformin and lactate, are used. The nurse identifies patients at risk, including those with hypotension, sepsis, heart failure, or on high-risk medications, monitors the lactate, the anion gap, blood pressure, urine output, and mental status, and reports a rising lactate or worsening acidemia promptly. The nurse assists with aggressive fluid resuscitation and vasopressor support as ordered, and coordinates with the critical care team. The patient is educated about the signs of a serious metabolic disturbance and the importance of reporting symptoms, and about medication risks, such as the need to hold metformin during acute illness in patients with kidney disease. Because lactic acidosis develops and resolves with the underlying condition, monitoring trends and treating the cause are the keys to management."},
  {"topic":"Fluid and Electrolyte Emergencies","title":"Monitoring Fluid Balance and Volume Status","body":"Accurate fluid balance monitoring is fundamental in the renal patient, whether the problem is acute kidney injury, chronic kidney disease, or the management of dialysis. The nurse measures and records all intake and output, including oral fluids, intravenous fluids, blood products, urine, drain output, and losses from vomiting or diarrhea, and tracks daily weights, because a weight change of one kilogram roughly equals one liter of fluid. The physical examination supplements the numeric record: the patient with fluid overload may show edema, elevated blood pressure, jugular venous distention, crackles on lung exam, and dyspnea, while the volume-depleted patient may show orthostatic hypotension, tachycardia, dry mucous membranes, and decreased skin turgor. Urine output is a key indicator of renal perfusion, and a decreasing output in the setting of acute kidney injury prompts urgent assessment of prerenal versus intrinsic causes. In the patient on hemodialysis, the interdialytic weight gain reflects the balance between fluid intake and urine output, and excessive gains are associated with hypertension and pulmonary edema, so the nurse educates the patient on fluid and sodium restriction and on weighing daily. In continuous renal replacement therapy or the critically ill patient, the hourly machine balance and the patient's hemodynamic response guide ultrafiltration. The nurse monitors vital signs, blood pressure, urine output, daily weights, and symptoms, and communicates these trends to the provider so that decisions about fluids, diuretics, and dialysis are grounded in reliable data. Documentation is meticulous because small changes in fluid balance can matter greatly in a patient with little renal reserve. Education focuses on the importance of tracking fluids and sodium at home, recognizing early signs of overload, and reporting weight gain, swelling, or shortness of breath. A systematic approach to fluid balance, combining measured intake and output, weights, clinical signs, and hemodynamics, keeps the patient euvolemic and prevents the complications of both overload and depletion."}
];
